%I A399626 #27 Oct 15 2026 18:50:45
%S A399626 0,0,0,0,0,0,2,2,6,7,33,157,980,5947,39472,285644,2133620,16643449,
%T A399626 140324469
%N A399626 Maximum, over all placements of 2 pawns on an n X n board, of the number of ways to place n+2 nonattacking queens on the remaining squares.
%C A399626 The pawns are blockers only: queens do not attack pawns and pawns do not attack queens. A pawn interrupts a line of attack, so two queens on the same row, column or diagonal do not attack each other if a pawn lies between them (the convention of A129551). This is the k = 2 case of the n+k queens problem (Chatham et al.); cf. A103330 (k = 1) and A129553 (k = 3).
%C A399626 a(n) <= A129551(n), with A129551(n) = Sum over all pawn placements of the number of queen placements.
%C A399626 For 11 <= n <= 19 the maximum is attained by a unique pawn placement up to symmetry, with both pawns adjacent to the center of the board: for n = 19 the pawns are on squares (8,8) and (9,10) in 0-based (row,column) coordinates; for n = 18 on (7,8) and (9,9); for n = 17 on (6,7) and (8,8).
%H A399626 R. D. Chatham, <a href="https://www.npluskqueens.info/">The N+k Queens Problem Page</a>.
%H A399626 R. D. Chatham, M. Doyle, G. H. Fricke, J. Reitmann, R. D. Skaggs and M. Wolff, <a href="https://www.npluskqueens.info/uploads/2/1/3/5/21355572/queenssep2.pdf">Independence and Domination Separation on Chessboard Graphs</a>, J. Combin. Math. Combin. Comput. 68 (2009), 3-17.
%e A399626 For n = 9 the pawn placement {(3,4),(4,3)} admits 6 placements of the 11 queens, and no placement admits more, so a(9) = 6.
%Y A399626 Cf. A129551, A129552, A399501.
%K A399626 nonn,hard,more
%O A399626 1,7
